# Refactoring oversized code

On a size-gate failure (file over ~300 lines, method over ~30 lines, more than 4 parameters, nesting deeper than 3,
or an oversized test file), a structure-guardrail failure (`src/UnitTests/BuildGates/Refactoring/`), or a
Refactoring rationale failure: **read and follow `docs/refactoring-oversized-code.md` before editing.**

- Diagnose first: name the smells and the domain concept trying to emerge.
- Use a preferred move from the playbook; any other move needs a written reason (`Other: <move>`).
- Never: `partial` types to dodge a limit, extension methods on our own types, static `*Helper`/`*Util` classes,
  numbered or vague names (`Part1`, `Extra`, `More`, `Misc`, `Manager2`), pass-through classes, splitting by line count.
- Tests: extract similar tests into their own fixtures in a category folder and namespace; every test method reads as
  visible steps (one well-named helper call each, no loops or branches, no whole-scenario helper); `[SetUp]` does
  infrastructure, plus shared arrange steps only in a `Given...` fixture; data in ObjectMother/builders, actions in
  small `*Steps` classes.
- Every extracted class gets its own fixture; delete the fixed file's line from the baseline (baselines only shrink).
- Fill in the pull request's **Refactoring plan**: the rationale table (Smell | Refactoring | New abstraction | Why), a
  Before and an After `mermaid` diagram (`classDiagram` or `flowchart`) per concept cluster that name every new
  production type, and the line-count table pasted from
  `pwsh scripts/refactoring/Test-RefactoringRationale.ps1 -BaseRef origin/master -WriteLineCountTable`.
